 [17592186524947 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524947 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524947 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524947 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524947 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524947 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524947 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524947 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f297313b-3683-5d45-a02e-e17a9c10bf9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524949 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa393c5b-69a5-51b3-a77d-1f39127b18dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524959 #edb/ref 1071 "The distance covered per game by a particular soccer player is uniformly distributed between $10\\,\\text{km}$\n and $13\\,\\text{km}$ inclusive. What is the probability that this player runs $12\\,\\text{km}$ or more in a randomly selected game?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04a40517-0aa4-5398-b54b-804112b670f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524959 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the distance covered by the player. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[10,13],$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\ge 12).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[10,13],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & 10\\le x\\le 13 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 12) & ={\\int }_{12}^{13}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{12}^{13}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{3}|}_{12}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{12}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524968 #edb/ref 1071 "The distance covered by a basketball player in an official match is uniformly distributed between $3$ and $4$ km, inclusive. What is the mean distance covered per match?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1aba9da0-33cd-5e33-a9f2-c4176a10b635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524968 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the distance covered by a randomly chosen basketball player. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[3,4],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[3,4],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3+4}{2}=3.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean distance covered is $3.5\\,\\text{km}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524969 #edb/ref 1071 "The battery duration from a full to an empty charge of a specific smartphone model is uniformly distributed between $20$ and $30$ hours, inclusive.  What is the expected duration of the smartphone battery?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e4d63bb-964b-5518-80b3-c4adef9c4173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524969 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly chosen battery. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[20,30],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[20,30],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{20+30}{2}=25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean battery duration is $25\\,\\text{hrs}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524977 #edb/ref 1071 "The minimum time it takes a plumber to fix a leaky pipe is $30$ minutes, and the maximum time is $110$ minutes.  Assuming that the plumber's time to fix a leaky pipe is uniformly distributed, what is the expected time to fix a pipe?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34de765e-8b52-5fc4-a07d-973bddb6d3ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524977 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time to fix a randomly chosen leaky pipe. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[30,110],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[30,110],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{30+110}{2}=70\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean time to fix the leaky pipe is $70\\,\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524995 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524995 #edb/ref 1071 "In an online poker tournament, the maximum loss is $10$ dollars, and the maximum win is $8$ dollars. Assuming that a player's earnings are uniformly distributed between $-10$ and $8,$ what is the probability that a randomly chosen player loses between $2$ and $8$ dollars?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75c2a299-1223-5668-b631-6cae0200f484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524995 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the earnings of a randomly chosen player. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[-10,8],$ and we wish to compute $P(-8\\le X\\le -2).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[-10,8],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & -10\\le x\\le 8 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-8\\le X\\le -2) & ={\\int }_{-8}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-8}^{-2}\\frac{1}{18}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{18}|}_{-8}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{18}-(-\\frac{8}{18}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525004 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525004 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525004 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525004 #edb/ref 1071 "The distance covered per game by a football player in an official match is uniformly distributed between $9$ km and $12$ km, inclusive. What is the variance in the distance covered by this player?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "881664f0-8e9c-5727-bbea-b101cf17a4b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525004 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the distance covered by the player. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[9,12],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[9,12],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(12-9)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{9}{12}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the distance covered by the football player is $\\frac{3}{4}\\,{\\text{km}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525018 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525018 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525018 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525018 #edb/ref 1071 "The minimum time it takes an electrician to fix a power outage is $20$ minutes, and the maximum time is $160$ minutes. Assuming that the time it takes to fix the power outage is uniformly distributed, what is the probability that a random power outage fix takes more than $30$ minutes but less than $60$ minutes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d9e7f8d-7ad5-5408-a4ca-eaab80943f96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525018 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time it takes to fix a random power outage. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[20,160],$ and we wish to compute $P(30<X<60).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[20,160],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{140}, & 20\\le x\\le 160 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(30<X<60) & ={\\int }_{30}^{60}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{30}^{60}\\frac{1}{140}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{140}|}_{30}^{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{140}-\\frac{30}{140} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525022 #edb/ref 1071 "The return on a risky investment can be negative (meaning that the investor loses money) or positive. Assuming that the return on a particular investment is uniformly distributed between $-7.5%$ and $12.5%$, what is the mean return of this investment?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a26697c2-b707-5c92-9d4e-fbd65d029113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525022 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the return on the investment. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[-7.5,12.5],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[-7.5,12.5],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{-7.5+12.5}{2}=2.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean return on the investment is $2.5%.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525027 #edb/ref 1071 "The duration of a tv program is uniformly distributed between $24$ and $36$ minutes, inclusive. What is the variance of the TV program duration?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad59302d-b27c-5dd6-8065-5abc6de41bf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525027 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the duration of a tv program. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[24,36],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[24,36],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(36-24)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{144}{12}=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the TV program duration is $12\\,{\\text{min}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525029 #edb/ref 1071 "A librarian has noticed that the minimum visiting time of a visitor is $0.5$ hours, whereas the maximum time is $6.5$ hours. If the visiting time is uniformly distributed, what is its variance?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b676f753-31e2-5fb5-a7e2-ab7b8253e6c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525029 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the visiting time of a randomly chosen visitor. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[0.5,6.5],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[0.5,6.5],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(6.5-0.5)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{36}{12}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance  is $3\\,{\\text{hr}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525049 #edb/ref 1071 "The height of water in a dam is uniformly distributed between $120$ and $195$ meters, inclusive. What is the mean height of the water?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dde847bc-5ddb-54de-88c5-81b18b2aa0d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525049 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the height of water in a random inspection of the dam. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[120,195],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[120,195],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{120+195}{2}=157.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean height of water in the dam is $157.5\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525052 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525052 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525052 #edb/ref 1071 "A computer program is designed so that it can randomly generate a real number between $0$ and $10,$ inclusive. What is the probability that the number generated is between $3$ and $7?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e042f50a-1181-570f-8ece-0feac63fb8cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525052 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number generated by the program. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[0,10],$ and we wish to compute $P(3<X<7).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[0,10],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<7) & ={\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{10}|}_{3}^{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525056 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525056 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525056 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525056 #edb/ref 1071 "The duration of a tv program is uniformly distributed between $52$ and $66$ minutes, inclusive. What is the probability that a randomly chosen program lasts $60$ minutes or less?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e55c9ea3-689a-5b3d-8e62-79deec4589d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525056 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the duration of the tv program. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[52,66],$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\le 60).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[52,66],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{14}, & 52\\le x\\le 66 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 60) & ={\\int }_{52}^{60}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{52}^{60}\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{14}|}_{52}^{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{14}-\\frac{52}{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525066 #edb/ref 1071 "A video game randomly generates an obstacle the player has to avoid. The obstacle's $x$-coordinate is uniformly distributed along a segment whose endpoints have $x$-coordinates of $16$ and $24.$ What is the variance of the $x$-coordinate of the obstacle?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fefe1132-ca93-5e93-921e-5c1fd45e5581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525066 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the randomly generated $x$-coordinate of the obstacle. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[16,24],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[16,24],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(24-16)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{64}{12}=\\frac{16}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the $x$-coordinate is $\\frac{16}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
